.TH LINAUDIT 1 "2026-11-12" "linaudit 0.3.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
linaudit \- read\-only security audit for Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B linaudit
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B linaudit
checks a Linux system (Debian, Ubuntu, RHEL and derivatives) for common security
problems and gives it a score from 1 (worst) to 10 (best), together with the list
of findings sorted by severity and how to fix each one.
.PP
It runs more than 100 checks in nine categories: accounts and sudo, SSH,
filesystem permissions, plaintext secrets, network and firewall, patching,
persistence and signs of compromise, kernel and service hardening, and common
server applications (Tomcat, Redis, Docker, PostgreSQL, Samba, Apache, nginx).
.PP
.B linaudit
is strictly read\-only: no check modifies the system, and it never prints the value
of a secret it finds, only the file and line. The only file it writes on its own
is the user's language preference (see
.BR LANGUAGE ).
.PP
Run it as root for a complete audit. Without root several checks cannot read what
they need; they are reported as not evaluated and the score is capped at 6.
.PP
In a terminal,
.B linaudit
opens an interactive interface: a welcome screen to choose the system profile,
live progress, and three tabs (Summary, Findings, Categories). Press
.B ?
for the keyboard shortcuts. In a pipe, in cron, or with
.BR \-\-plain ,
it prints a text report instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " PROFILE"
System profile:
.BR server ,
.BR desktop ,
.B container
or
.BR auto .
Without it, the welcome screen asks (or the profile is auto\-detected when there
is no terminal). See
.BR PROFILES .
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-no\-interactive
Do not ask for the profile even with a terminal; use auto\-detection.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Print a text report instead of opening the interactive interface.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the report as JSON, for a central collector.
.TP
.BI \-\-output " FILE"
Save the report to
.I FILE
(created with mode 600) instead of printing it.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Also list the passed checks (in the interactive interface, start by showing all checks).
.TP
.B \-\-fast
Skip the full filesystem sweeps (SUID/SGID, world\-writable and orphaned files,
package integrity). Takes seconds instead of minutes.
.TP
.BI \-\-category " LIST"
Run only these categories, separated by commas (for example
.BR ssh,accounts ).
The score is then partial. See
.BR \-\-list\-categories .
.TP
.BI \-\-waivers " FILE"
Read an extra waivers file, besides
.IR /etc/linaudit/waivers.conf .
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-score " N"
Exit with status 1 if the score is lower than
.I N
(1 to 10), or if nothing could be evaluated. Useful in cron, CI and monitoring.
.TP
.BI \-\-lang " LANG"
Interface language:
.B en
or
.BR es .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Do not use colors.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-profiles
List the profiles, mark the one detected on this system, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-categories
List the categories and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH PROFILES
A profile never hides a check: it either changes its severity or marks it as not
applicable, and says so in the finding's details.
.TP
.B server
Full criteria: every check with its original severity.
.TP
.B desktop
For personal computers. Relaxes checks meant for corporate servers (legal banner,
password expiry, log forwarding, fail2ban, services such as cups or avahi) and
lowers the firewall check from HIGH to MEDIUM.
.TP
.B container
What the host or the container runtime manages (clock, AppArmor/SELinux, auditd,
firewall, logs, kernel parameters) is marked as not applicable.
.PP
The profile is taken, in this order, from
.BR \-\-profile ,
.BR LINAUDIT_PROFILE ,
.B profile
in
.IR /etc/linaudit/config.toml ,
the welcome screen, or auto\-detection.
.SH SCORE
Each check weighs according to its severity: CRITICAL 10, HIGH 6, MEDIUM 3, LOW 1,
INFO 0. A passed check adds its full weight, a warning half of it, a failure
nothing; not applicable and waived checks do not count. The result is scaled to
1\(en10 and then capped by the open findings: 3 or more critical caps it at 3, 2
critical at 4, 1 critical or 5 or more high at 6, and 1 to 4 high at 8. If more
than 10% of the weight could not be evaluated (usually because of running without
root), the score is capped at 6 and the evaluation is marked as incomplete.
.SH WAIVERS
Findings that are accepted on purpose (for example, a dedicated perimeter firewall)
can be recorded in
.IR /etc/linaudit/waivers.conf ,
one per line:
.PP
.RS
.EX
NET\-001   # Dedicated perimeter firewall. Ticket INF\-1234
.EE
.RE
.PP
A waived check is not scored and is listed in the report with its reason.
.SH LANGUAGE
The interface, reports and help are available in English and Spanish. The language
is taken, in this order, from
.B \-\-lang
or
.BR LINAUDIT_LANG ,
the user's preference in
.IR ~/.config/linaudit/config.toml ,
.B language
in
.IR /etc/linaudit/config.toml ,
the locale
.RB ( LANGUAGE ", " LC_ALL ", " LC_MESSAGES ", " LANG ),
and finally English.
.PP
The first time the interactive interface opens without a chosen language, the
welcome screen asks for it and saves it in the user's preference file. To choose
again, delete that file, or press
.B i
on the welcome screen. With
.BR sudo (8),
the preference of the invoking user is used.
.PP
Check IDs and JSON keys are the same in every language.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LINAUDIT_PROFILE
Same as
.BR \-\-profile .
.TP
.B LINAUDIT_LANG
Same as
.BR \-\-lang .
.TP
.B LINAUDIT_CONFIG
Use this file instead of
.IR /etc/linaudit/config.toml .
.TP
.B LINAUDIT_SCAN_TIMEOUT
Maximum seconds for each filesystem sweep (default 120).
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Do not use colors.
.TP
.B LINAUDIT_DEBUG
Set to 1 to show the internal error of a check category that failed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/linaudit/config.toml
System\-wide settings: default
.B profile
and
.BR language .
.TP
.I /etc/linaudit/waivers.conf
Documented waivers.
.TP
.I ~/.config/linaudit/config.toml
The user's language preference.
.TP
.I /var/lib/linaudit/latest.json
Latest report written by
.BR linaudit.service ,
which
.B linaudit.timer
runs weekly once enabled
.RB ( "systemctl enable \-\-now linaudit.timer" ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The audit ran.
.TP
.B 1
The score is lower than
.BR \-\-min\-score ,
or nothing could be evaluated.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 130
Cancelled by the user.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
sudo linaudit
sudo linaudit \-\-plain \-\-profile server
sudo linaudit \-\-json \-\-output /var/lib/linaudit/latest.json
sudo linaudit \-\-fast \-\-min\-score 7 \-\-profile server
.EE
.SH NOTES
Reports describe the weaknesses of a machine: keep them private, and send them to a
collector only over an authenticated, encrypted channel.
.PP
Several checks are heuristic and can report false positives; findings are things
to review, not verdicts.
.B linaudit
does not replace a CIS/STIG compliance audit, a forensic analysis or an EDR.
.SH SEE ALSO
More information: <https://lexiworks.dev/linaudit>
